# Color applet: the palette, Application Workspace given another basic colour (Tab, Up, Space),
# Tab x4 to "Save Scheme" (the colour grids want characters, so mnemonics do not work from them),
# the Save Scheme dialog (27), "test" typed over the proposed name, Enter: CONTROL.INI gets the
# scheme at once; Esc then cancels the Color dialog, so WIN.INI and [current] stay as they were.
# Same keys as arch311-ref/scn/color-save.scn; compare in 16 colours (W16_COLORS=16). The reference
# caught the Save Scheme edit's caret off, so frames 04 and 05 are shot without it.
# regress: W16_COLORS=16 sh tools/run-cp-test.sh "$TEST" "$OUT"
# compare: color-save/02.png 02.png active ignore 320 240 332 260
# compare: color-save/03.png 03.png active ignore 320 240 332 260
# compare: color-save/04.png 04.png active ignore 320 240 332 260
# compare: color-save/05.png 05.png active ignore 320 240 332 260
# compare: color-save/06.png 06.png active ignore 320 240 332 260
# ini: CONTROL.INI [color schemes] test=C0C0C0,400000,FFFFFF,0,FFFFFF,0,800000,FFFFFF,FFFFFF,C0C0C0,C0C0C0,0,C0C0C0,C0C0C0,808080,0,C0C0C0,800000,FFFFFF,0,FFFFFF
# ini: CONTROL.INI current color schemes=Windows Default
# ini: WIN.INI colors AppWorkspace=
sleep 400
shot shots/01.png
key enter
key tab
key space
key down
key tab
key up
key space
sleep 400
shot shots/02.png
key tab
key tab
key tab
key tab
sleep 400
shot shots/03.png
key space
sleep 400
shot shots/04.png
key t
key e
key s
key t
sleep 400
shot shots/05.png
key enter
sleep 400
shot shots/06.png
key esc
sleep 400
shot shots/07.png
quit
